   "id": "04-s04-how-it-works",
   "scene": 4,
   "file": "04-s04-how-it-works.mp3",
   "align": "04-s04-how-it-works.align.json",
   "text": "Some human echolocators produce sharp clicks using their tongue. Sound travels through air at roughly three hundred and forty-three meters per second. If a wall is two meters away, the sound can travel there and back in about twelve milliseconds. That's incredibly fast. Your brain doesn't need to hear a completely separate echo each time. Small differences in timing, loudness and sound quality can provide useful spatial information.",
